#iterating through a list using For loop
states = ["Washington", "Virginia", "Maryland"]

for x in states:
    print(x)
    
# x is just the iteration variable, best practice to rename
# it in a reasonable way, relatable to the contents

for state in states:
    print(state)
    
'''
the variable name you use here does not matter to the computer,
but if you give it a descriptive name, it will help you
and others who look at your code understand what's going on. 
'''

#can modify the list as well

for state in states:
    state = state.lower()
        print(state)
#to find whether an item exists in a list with the in keyword

'''
The in keyword can be used on its own, without a for loop.
You can use it to construct a conditional expression that 
evaluates as Boolean True or False.
You can also use the not Boolean operator along with it, to
flip its return value from True to False or vice versa.
'''

print("Washington" in states)
print("Tennesse" in states)
print("Washington" not in states)

